Evaluated cross-compilation paths without local Linux infrastructure

No build directory on the router and GCC 8.4.0 is userspace-only — you cannot compile kernel modules in-place. Here's how we get around it without VMware.
Three paths, pick whichever you have access to
Option A — Google Cloud Shell (fastest, zero install)

Go to shell.cloud.google.com in a browser (needs a Google account, free). You get an Ubuntu VM instantly. Then skip to the build commands below.
Option B — WSL2 on Windows (if you have Windows 10/11)

Open PowerShell as admin:
powershell

wsl --install -d Ubuntu

Reboot, open Ubuntu from the Start menu. Takes 5 minutes. Then run the build commands below.
Option C — GitHub Actions (fully automated, no Linux at all)

This is the cleanest path if you have a GitHub account — the CI machine compiles it and you download the .ko files as a zip artifact. Steps below.
Option C — GitHub Actions (recommended if no Linux available)

Step 1 — Get the source onto GitHub from the router.

First check if git is on the router:
sh

opkg update && opkg install git git-http ca-bundle

Then push to a new GitHub repo:
sh

cd /opt/dsl-s-wan
git init
git add .
git commit -m "dsl-s arm64 port"
git remote add origin https://github.com/YOUR_USERNAME/dsl-s-wan.git
git push -u origin main
# it will prompt for GitHub username + a Personal Access Token as password

To create a token: GitHub → Settings → Developer Settings → Personal access tokens → Tokens (classic) → New → tick repo scope.

Step 2 — Run the workflow.

Go to your repo on GitHub → Actions tab → Build DSL-S Modules → Run workflow → green button.

It will take about 10–15 minutes. The job downloads the OpenWrt snapshot SDK, runs make target/linux/prepare to get the exact kernel headers, cross-compiles all five .ko files, and packages them.

Step 3 — Download the artifact.

When the green checkmark appears, click the workflow run → scroll to Artifacts → download dsl-s-modules-arm64-openwrt-1.zip.

Step 4 — Deploy to router.
sh

# From wherever you downloaded the artifact zip
scp rom_attest.ko fault_reload.ko stack_guard.ko wan_ingress.ko elf_sentinel.ko \
    root@<router-ip>:/lib/modules/6.12.80/extra/

ssh root@<router-ip>
mkdir -p /lib/modules/6.12.80/extra
depmod -a

Build commands for Options A and B (Cloud Shell or WSL2)
bash

sudo apt-get update && sudo apt-get install -y \
  build-essential wget zstd libssl-dev libelf-dev \
  bc flex bison python3 rsync gawk git

# Download the snapshot SDK (~200 MB)
wget https://downloads.openwrt.org/snapshots/targets/qualcommax/ipq807x/\
openwrt-sdk-qualcommax-ipq807x_gcc-14.3.0_musl.Linux-x86_64.tar.zst

mkdir openwrt-sdk
tar --zstd -xf openwrt-sdk-*.tar.zst -C openwrt-sdk --strip-components=1

# Pull the source from the router
scp -r root@<router-ip>:/opt/dsl-s-wan ./dsl-s-wan

# Get kernel headers (5–10 min, downloads kernel source once)
cd openwrt-sdk
./scripts/feeds update base
make target/linux/prepare -j$(nproc) ARCH=arm64

# Find the paths
KDIR=$(find build_dir -name ".config" -path "*linux-qualcommax*" -exec dirname {} \; | head -1)
CROSS=$(find staging_dir -name "aarch64-openwrt-linux-musl-gcc" | head -1 | sed 's/gcc$//')
cd ..

# Build
mkdir output
for mod in rom_attest fault_reload stack_guard wan_ingress; do
  make -C dsl-s-wan/$mod ARCH=arm64 CROSS_COMPILE=$CROSS KDIR=$KDIR
  cp dsl-s-wan/$mod/$mod.ko output/
done
make -C dsl-s-wan/../elf-sentinel-suite ARCH=arm64 CROSS_COMPILE=$CROSS KDIR=$KDIR
cp elf-sentinel-suite/elf_sentinel.ko output/ 2>/dev/null || true

# Push to router
scp output/*.ko root@<router-ip>:/lib/modules/6.12.80/extra/

The updated zip above now includes the GitHub Actions workflow at .github/workflows/build-arm64-modules.yml and the missing elf_sentinel/Makefile that was absent from the original. You can push that whole zip to a GitHub repo directly if you want. Once you have the .ko files on the router, come back and I'll give you the exact insmod sequence in the right order with the right parameters.
