Here's what changed in stack_guard.c and why each fix matters:

The regs->dx read in mprotect_entry_handler was the most dangerous one — it would have silently compiled on ARM64 since struct pt_regs has a dx field alias on some configs, but it would have read the wrong register entirely, meaning prot would be garbage and the PROT_EXEC check would never fire. SG_PROT_ARG(regs) maps to regs->regs[2] on ARM64 which is where AAPCS64 puts the third argument.

The CET blocks were already correctly guarded by #ifdef CONFIG_X86_USER_SHADOW_STACK so static_cpu_has(X86_FEATURE_SHSTK) never reaches the compiler on ARM64 — those were safe already. The only real problem there was the #else message saying "recompile with CONFIG_X86_USER_SHADOW_STACK=y" which is nonsense advice on an ARM64 router. That now emits the actual status via SG_SHADOW_STACK_STATUS which will print unavailable on the IPQ807x, which is accurate.
