Searched the web

The picture is completely clear now. This is not a kernel module problem at all. The diagnosis:

The Q6 WCSS DSP is crash-looping at #404449. That crash counter means it's been failing hundreds of thousands of times. The firmware-2.bin is entirely missing from /lib/firmware/ath11k/IPQ8074/hw2.0/ — you only have board-2.bin. The Q6 DSP loads its operating firmware from the q6_fw.* segment files (collectively packaged as firmware-2.bin in the split format), and without it the remoteproc subsystem boots the DSP into nothing, it faults immediately, remoteproc recovers it, and it faults again. Infinitely.

No kernel modules need to be compiled. ath11k.ko and ath11k_ahb.ko are already present and correct. The entire fix is delivering the missing firmware files.

On Kali, run this now:
bash

# Clone linux-firmware — only the ath11k IPQ8074 directory, no full repo needed
cd ~/ctw-build
git init ath11k-fw && cd ath11k-fw
git remote add origin https://gitlab.com/kernel-firmware/linux-firmware.git
git config core.sparseCheckout true
echo "ath11k/IPQ8074/hw2.0/" > .git/info/sparse-checkout
git pull --depth=1 origin main

ls -lh ath11k/IPQ8074/hw2.0/

That should show you board-2.bin, firmware-2.bin, and the q6_fw.* segment files. Then deploy:
bash

# Deploy to router
scp ath11k/IPQ8074/hw2.0/firmware-2.bin \
    ath11k/IPQ8074/hw2.0/m3_fw.* \
    ath11k/IPQ8074/hw2.0/q6_fw.* \
    root@192.168.1.1:/lib/firmware/ath11k/IPQ8074/hw2.0/

Then on the router:
bash

# Verify files landed
ls -lh /lib/firmware/ath11k/IPQ8074/hw2.0/

# Trigger reload — rmmod/insmod or simply reboot
# If ath11k is built-in, reboot is required
reboot

After reboot, check:
bash

dmesg | grep -E "ath11k|q6v5|remoteproc" | head -20
iw phy
ip link show

A successful load looks like ath11k c000000.wifi: IPQ8074 hw2.0 AHB WLAN.HK... followed by phy entries. The crash loop stops the moment firmware-2.bin is present.

The AX89X-specific board file is the second thing to check. The board-2.bin already on the router is a container of calibration blobs keyed by board ID. When ath11k probes, it reports its board ID via QMI and the driver extracts the matching entry. If the AX89X's board ID isn't in there, it falls back to a generic entry which may cause poor RF performance or regulatory issues even after the crash loop stops. After the reboot paste the dmesg output — the board_id= line will tell us whether it matched a real entry or defaulted to 0xFF.

