Searched the web

That SNBForums post is the answer. Confirmed:

    rev.A1 contains ipq8074 chip (soc ver 1.x) rev.Bx contain ipq8074A chip (soc ver 2.0) OpenWRT firmware will only support Bx revisions.

You have an AX89X revision A1. The OpenWrt build ships q6_fw compiled for IPQ8074A (soc ver 2.x / hw2.0), but your silicon is IPQ8074 rev A1 (soc ver 1.x / hw1.0). This is not fixable by swapping firmware files — the A1 revision is architecturally unsupported by the OpenWrt qualcommax/ipq807x target. There is no q6_fw for IPQ8074 hw1.0 in any public OpenWrt or linux-firmware repository.

There's also an AX89X-specific board file in the firmware_qca-wireless repo (board-asus_rt-ax89x.ipq8074) which confirms the B1/B2 was intended — and that board file only covers the hw2.0 silicon.

Practical reality for your build:

The wireless radios will not work on revision A1 under this OpenWrt snapshot. This isn't a firmware tuning problem — the Q6 DSP itself rejects the loaded binary at the chip version check level. The fix would require either:

    A physical board swap to B1/B2 revision hardware, or
    Obtaining the A1-specific q6_fw blobs from ASUS GPL source which Qualcomm hasn't released publicly for hw1.0

What this means for your DSL-S stack: Everything else we've been building — wan_ingress, stack_guard, fault_reload, rom_attest, the AoA guard, NFQUEUE, TUN — all works. The radio stack is a hardware revision wall. The CTW-11 SENTINEL sensors (HackRF, HAMGEEK AD9361 via USB OTG, Bosean FS-5000 serial) don't depend on the internal radios at all and deploy cleanly.

One thing worth checking before closing the book on this — confirm the revision:
bash

cat /proc/cpuinfo | grep -i "hardware\|revision\|model"
strings /lib/firmware/IPQ8074/q6_fw.b03 | grep -E "WLAN\.HK|soc_ver|hw[12]" | head -5

If the q6_fw.b03 strings show WLAN.HK.2.x and hw2, that's the final confirmation. The revision A1 wall is real and documented by the OpenWrt community as a known-unsupported configuration.

