Binwalk extracted nothing useful — it hit the TRX but couldn't unpack the inner filesystem. Install the missing tools and do it manually:
bash

cd /opt/xfer

# Install what binwalk actually needs
apt-get install -y jefferson ubi-reader mtd-utils lzma xz-utils lz4 \
    firmware-mod-kit squashfs-tools 2>/dev/null | tail -3

# TRX format: 28-byte header then concatenated images
# Read the header to find partition offsets
python3 -c "
import struct
with open('RT-AX89X_3.0.0.4_388_33744-g03a793e.trx','rb') as f:
    hdr = f.read(28)
magic, length, crc, flags, ver = struct.unpack('<IIIHI', hdr[:18])
offsets = struct.unpack('<4I', hdr[12:28])
print(f'Magic: {magic:#010x}')
print(f'Length: {length}')
print(f'Offsets: {offsets}')
"

Paste the offsets output. TRX stores kernel at offset[0], rootfs at offset[1], and sometimes a third partition at offset[2] — the WLAN firmware blob typically lives in the rootfs squashfs under /lib/firmware/. Once we have the offsets we dd the rootfs partition directly and unsquashfs it. Three commands after that.

